__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
# own-history distribution cache (§D) — derived from data/, keyed by its hash
backend/.cache/
# the simulation's loader cache — pickled row tables the workbook loaders write
# next to the workbooks (irs_pricer/loaders/cache.py). Derived, per-machine,
# and rebuilt whenever core/data_watch.py notices a workbook changed.
data/.cache/
# Excel's lock file, present while the workbook is OPEN (`~$irsdata.xlsx`).
# It appeared as an untracked file during a data refresh and a `git add -A`
# would have committed it. It is not data and it disappears when Excel closes.
~$*.xls*
# The simulation workbooks, which came over with irs_pricer. UNTRACKED, unlike
# this repo's own irsdata/bokbaserate — they total ~50 MB and `Credit Matrix
# Data.xlsx` alone is 42 MB. xlsx is already compressed, so git cannot delta
# them: every refresh would store a fresh full copy and the history would grow
# by 50 MB a day. simulation_project ignored them for exactly this reason and
# that rationale survives the move. The owner drops them into data/ directly;
# core/data_watch.py notices the change and clears the derived caches.
# Archives that APPEAR IN data/ ON THEIR OWN. `AS_data.zip` (24 MB, holding
# four of the workbooks) showed up during a test run on 2026-08-07, alongside
# three workbooks going missing — including a tracked one. Nothing in this repo
# creates a zip; the correlation was with `NetClient6 Agent Task`, a machine
# agent outside this project. Unexplained, and left alone at the owner's
# direction (the workbooks move to MySQL shortly, which ends the question).
# Ignored so `git add -A` cannot sweep one into a commit.
data/*.zip
data/BOK Base Rate.xlsx
data/Call Rate Data.xlsx
data/Credit Matrix Data.xlsx
data/Portfolio Data.xlsx
data/True Data.xlsx

.vercel
.env*
